# Rig performance measurement

Scope: every autorig, deformation, LOD, simulation and rig package change.
Read `maya_tools/docs/rig_performance_criteria.md` for current acceptance and
`maya_tools/docs/rig_optimization_roadmap_20261015.md` for implementation order.
Based on Guido Gonzalez's 2026 rig study, sections 3-5, 7, 10 and 12.

- Optimize measured critical-path work. Scene node counts and polygon counts are
  inventory, not proof of a frame-time bottleneck. Record the hypothesis first.
- Use interactive Maya with Viewport 2.0 for viewport/GPU acceptance. Batch Maya
  can validate geometry and inventory, but cannot certify interactive FPS.
- New rigs default to conservative Parallel CPU with cache OFF after lazy DG
  construction. GPU/cache require scene-specific geometry and invalidation gates;
  check fallback/Safe Mode, scheduling and GPU membership under animated load.
- Benchmark temporal playback and control manipulation separately. Exercise body,
  face, correctives, wrinkles and relevant contacts; a static rig is not a test.
- Compare DG, Parallel CPU, Parallel GPU and warm Cached Playback. Use Serial
  separately to diagnose geometry differences. Record cache fill/invalidation.
- Fix scene/build hashes, Maya/API/plugins, hardware, units, FPS, camera, viewport
  size, shading, frame range and evaluators. Compare identical workload variants.
- Use a warm-up pass, discard at least 8 initial frames per timed playback pass,
  and collect at least 3 measured passes. Report median, p95, max, samples and
  dispersion. An improvement inside measured noise is inconclusive.
- Time without mesh extraction, validation, screenshots or profiler recording;
  capture those separately. Forced refresh includes submission overhead and is
  not isolated GPU execution. Do not label geometry-snapshot timings pure FPS.
- Verify visible output through every DAG ancestor; invisible detail is not a
  valid quality-matched baseline. Preserve Smooth Mesh Preview settings.
- Policy: Animation playback p95 <= 1000 / shot FPS ms (41.67 ms at 24 FPS),
  with a preferred 33.33 ms target for headroom. These are project acceptance
  targets, not Edward measurements. Record interaction latency separately.
- Report actual GPU/CPU chains, reasons and downstream consumers. Aim for >90%
  GPU residency of primary deformed vertices; document measured exceptions for
  Bifrost/CFX. This diagnostic target is not a substitute for frame-time quality.
- Preserve baseline geometry: default max error <= 1e-4 scene units on matching
  topology, plus contact/IK-FK/space-switch checks and forward/reverse sampling.
  LOD comparisons require surface/landmark checks, not matching vertex indices.
- Use versioned trial scenes and reports. Restore keys, channels, connections,
  frame, selection, evaluator/cache/profiler state and modified state on failure.
- Reject regressions outside measured noise, missing pose channels, unstable
  topology, stale cache or lost artistic data. Validate two rebuilds, native scene
  reopen and SKC/preset round-trips before accepting a structural replacement.
- Do not transfer the study's speedups, 16.4 ms fitted intercept, GPU thresholds
  or subsystem costs to Edward without a controlled measurement.
- Compare cache and GPU separately to accepted CPU/DG geometry. Edward's later
  artist snapshot cache matched CPU in the tested scope while uncached GPU did
  not. Do not transfer that approval to the earlier regional scene or all edits.
- Bypass omits computation AND deformation; deltas are marginal/nonadditive.
  Inclusive CPU is not wall time. Native payload sizes overlap and are not RAM.
- Check normals/UV/materials and joint matrices as well as positions. Benchmark
  native-reopened binding cleanup separately from the post-edit state. Preserve
  tiny nonzero weights, DQ masks and matching skin/corrective order on SKC import.
  Retain cache/export failures as evidence; fast completed samples do not erase
  a hung warmup or approve a default profile.
- Profile build latency separately from animation. Keep optional health audits
  outside normal builds; never query native GPU partitions in automatic audits.
- Verify model-cache invalidation, file hashes and source equivalence.
